<ArticleLayout
	title="One Analysis Cycle, Step by Step — Semantic Signal"
	description="What the engine does every four hours: CCXT market data, 79 Numba-compiled indicators, a 1920x1080 chart for a multimodal model, and the checks that run outside the prompt."
	meta="Article · first published August 2026 · updated October 2026"
>
	<span slot="title">One analysis cycle, step by step</span>
	<p slot="subtitle" class="subtitle">What happens between two four-hour candles, in the order it happens — and which parts of the decision the code enforces rather than the prompt.</p>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>Timing</h2>
		<p>The engine runs one cycle per four-hour candle. In the 48 cycles recorded between 2026-10-19 and 2026-10-26 the median cycle took <strong>266 seconds</strong> and cost <strong>$0.0121</strong> in model usage — almost all of it spent waiting on the multimodal model, not on the local work. That is a small, single-instrument window, and it is the only latency figure this site quotes.</p>
		<p>The order of stages is fixed:</p>
		<ol>
			<li>fetch market data and account state;</li>
			<li>compute the indicator stack;</li>
			<li>render the chart image;</li>
			<li>collect news and fundamentals;</li>
			<li>retrieve trade memory;</li>
			<li>call the model and parse its JSON;</li>
			<li>validate claims, size the position and apply the guards;</li>
			<li>record the decision and route it to the executor.</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>1. Market data</h2>
		<p>One cycle pulls, through CCXT: <strong>999 OHLCV candles</strong> on the configured timeframe, a <strong>50-level order book</strong>, the funding rate, 24-hour volume and the last 500 trades. The order book is there because price and volume alone do not show where resting orders are — depth by level, bid/ask imbalance and spread are computed from it before anything is sent to the model.</p>
		<p>This is a single-exchange view of a market that trades on many venues. The bot does not aggregate books across exchanges, so its picture of liquidity is as good as one venue's book.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>2. Indicators</h2>
		<p><code>src/indicators/</code> holds <strong>79 functions compiled with Numba</strong> (<code>@njit(cache=True)</code>) — EMA, ADX, Supertrend, Ichimoku, Parabolic SAR, CMF, OBV, RSI, support/resistance and volatility work. The same functions appear 116 times across <code>src/</code>, and there are no external technical-analysis dependencies.</p>
		<p>Two reasons for that choice: the numbers are computed deterministically and can be re-checked line by line, and a model's numeric claims can be compared against values the engine already has. Indicator values describe the past; the entry decision does not follow from any single threshold.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>3. The chart image</h2>
		<p>Plotly renders a <strong>1920×1080 candlestick chart</strong> with SMA, RSI, CMF and OBV panels plus swing-point annotations. The PNG goes to the multimodal model next to the numeric stack, so it gets both a picture and the numbers behind it.</p>
		<p class="caption">Chart export (Plotly + Kaleido) is the least reliable local step. It runs in a thread with a hard timeout and three retries; if it fails, the cycle continues text-only instead of aborting. Code: <code>src/analyzer/pattern_engine/chart_generator.py</code>.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>4. News and fundamentals</h2>
		<p>Up to <strong>5 articles</strong> are read from RSS feeds (CoinDesk, CoinTelegraph, Decrypt, CryptoSlate) with <code>feedparser</code> and <code>defusedxml</code>, optionally enriched with the article body through Crawl4AI and trafilatura. Market fundamentals come from CoinGecko and DeFiLlama, and the mood index from Alternative.me. Relevance scoring is local; no sentiment API is paid for.</p>
		<p>X/Twitter is not included: without paid authentication it is not reliably scrapeable, so the bot runs on Reddit and RSS instead of describing a feed it cannot read. RSS headlines vary in quality, and the model is told that this block is context rather than evidence.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>5. Trade memory</h2>
		<p>The prompt carries the <strong>3 nearest past trades</strong>, win rate and average P&amp;L over the <strong>20 nearest trades</strong>, the <strong>3 most relevant learned rules</strong> and up to <strong>5 recent post-mortems</strong>. Prompt size is capped by <code>[prompt_budget]</code>: 100,000 estimated tokens per request, 16,384 tokens reserved for the image, and lower-priority blocks dropped whole when the budget is exceeded. <a href="/articles/vector-memory/">The memory article</a> covers what is stored and retrieved.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>6. The model call and the prompt</h2>
		<p>The production default is DeepSeek's flash model (<code>provider = deepseek</code>, <code>deepseek-flash</code>); Google Gemini 3.8 Flash, OpenRouter, a local LM Studio server and BlockRun sit in the fallback chain, and a cycle in which every provider fails becomes HOLD rather than an exception (<code>src/managers/provider_orchestrator.py</code>). Each provider call is retried with a named reason, so the log says whether a reply was empty, cut by the output cap, blocked by a content filter or malformed.</p>
		<p>The model returns structured JSON: signal, confidence, entry, stop, target, size, reasoning, and an explicit invalidation condition — the price or indicator state that would prove the thesis wrong. That invalidation step is a <strong>prompt instruction</strong>, not a tested gate: a model can write a vague one, or skip the discipline the prompt asks for.</p>
		<p>The expected-value rule works the same way. <code>EV = (win rate × average win) − ((1 − win rate) × average loss) − fees</code> is injected as a decision rule, computed from the bot's own closed trades, with fees taken from the configured <code>transaction_fee_percent</code> (0.075% per side, so a round trip costs twice that). The repository does not reject a signal on arithmetic alone.</p>
		<p>Prompt construction, including that rule and the falsification request, lives in <code>src/analyzer/prompts/template_manager.py</code>; the fee arithmetic is <code>src/analyzer/formatters/ev_formatter.py</code>.</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>7. What the code enforces instead</h2>
		<p>A smaller, testable set of checks runs outside the model:</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Configured symbol guard</strong> — outside the configured pair nothing is traded (<code>src/trading/guards/configured_symbol.py</code>);</li>
			<li><strong>Position-size cap</strong> — a requested size above <code>MAX_POSITION_SIZE</code> is rejected, and a missing or invalid size falls back to risk-manager sizing (<code>src/trading/guards/max_position_size.py</code>);</li>
			<li><strong>Entry R/R policy</strong> — one resolver, <code>resolve_entry_rr_floor()</code> in <code>src/trading/rr_policy.py</code>, takes the stricter of the configured <code>min_rr_entry</code> and the brain's learned threshold. It starts at <code>0.0</code>, so there is no fixed 1.5 floor by default, and the learned value can only raise it;</li>
			<li><strong>Claim validation</strong> — reported trend strength is compared with computed ADX (<code>src/analyzer/trend_validator.py</code>), and pattern quality is replaced by a deterministic scorer (<code>src/analyzer/pattern_quality_scorer.py</code>);</li>
			<li><strong>Dynamic SL/TP</strong> — stop and target distances scale with ATR and the regime profile, not with a percentage typed into a config;</li>
			<li><strong>Fail closed</strong> — if governance or risk validation cannot decide safely, no order is produced.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<h2>What this does not prove</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>A working pipeline says nothing about returns. It was built to be inspectable, not to be profitable by construction.</li>
			<li>Prompt-level rules (expected value, falsification) are instructions, not constraints. Only the guards and the R/R policy are enforced by code.</li>
			<li>The market view is single-venue and single-instrument by design; the cycle has no cross-exchange or cross-asset context.</li>
			<li>The latency and cost figures come from one week of recorded cycles and will not match other periods or providers.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

